<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kruševo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Krushevo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kruševo is a mountain town in the south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, at about 1,350 metres one of the highest towns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. A prosperous trading centre of Slav, Aromanian and Greek-oriented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> families under Ottoman rule, it was seized by insurgents in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilinden-preobrazhenie-uprising">Ilinden Uprising</a> of 1903, who proclaimed the short-lived Kruševo Republic. The Ilinden memorial, the Makedonium, stands above the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krusevo.jpg" alt="Kruševo" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milo van Kovacevic · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kru%C5%A1evo_northern_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Kruševo comes from the Slavic word for pear and means place of pear trees. The town lies at more than 1,350 metres above sea level, overlooking the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a>, 33 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a> and 53 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a>. It is the highest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and one of the highest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, and the seat of Kruševo Municipality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was first part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> in the 9th century and was taken back by the Byzantines in the 11th. It then fell to the short-lived Lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a> in the 14th century, before coming under Ottoman rule in 1395. A large part of the Macedonian population descends from the Mijaks, a Macedonian subgroup who came from Lazaropole and settled beside Aromanians by the middle of the 18th century. Orthodox Albanian refugees, fleeing the crises of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, also settled there, often in groups of families led by a priest.</p><p>In the 19th century Kruševo grew into a commercial centre with links across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and beyond, and local merchants such as the Nitsiotas brothers and five other companies were active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Bulgarian, Greek and Romanian schools operated in the town, the Romanian one opening in 1876. Early in the 20th century the Bulgarian geographer Vasil Kanchov counted 4,950 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a>, who are Aromanians, and 400 Christian Albanians.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilinden-preobrazhenie-uprising">Ilinden Uprising</a> of 1903 the rebels proclaimed the short-lived Kruševo Republic. Its leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-karev">Nikola Karev</a>, formed a council with twenty members from each of the town's three main groups: Slavic speakers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevo-manifesto">Kruševo Manifesto</a> called on all the people of Macedonia, whatever their nationality or religion, to fight together against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> for an independent Macedonian republic. When the uprising was suppressed, the Ottoman army almost completely destroyed the town. The band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pitu-guli">Pitu Guli</a> tried to defend it at Mečkin Kamen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 4,104 inhabitants, of whom 74.4 per cent were Macedonians and 21.1 per cent Aromanians. Since 2006 Macedonian and Aromanian have been the town's official languages, and Kruševo is the only place where Aromanian has any official status. The town is known for its 19th-century Ottoman architecture. Mečkin Kamen and the Makedonium monument commemorate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilinden-preobrazhenie-uprising">Ilinden Uprising</a>, and on 2 August each year Kruševo is one of two sites where the Macedonian Day of the Republic is observed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kruševo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rebels under Nikola Karev and Pitu Guli seized <b>Kruševo</b>, a prosperous mountain town of Slav, Aromanian and Greek-oriented Orthodox families, and proclaimed a provisional government.</blockquote><a href="/state/krusevo-republic">Kruševo Republic</a></li></ul></section>
